.\" Hints to aqr's compressor by path (.aqr/attributes), in mdoc(7). What is read is
.\" crates/aqr-compress/src/{attributes,hints}.rs and crates/aqr/src/attributes.rs; the
.\" parameters of formats are in crates/aqr-compress/src/recompressors.rs. Keep them together.
.Dd November 27, 2026
.Dt AQRATTRIBUTES 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm aqrattributes
.Nd hints to aqr of what files are, by their paths
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa .aqr/attributes
.Pp
.Nm aqr
.Fl -attributes Ar file
.Ar ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr aqr 1
finds what a file is by its first bytes and by its name.
Data of no header, streams inside other data and files of common extensions
.Pf ( Pa .bin ,
.Pa .dat )
are missed so.
A file of attributes tells
.Nm aqr ,
by pattern of paths, the format of such files, their kind, the formats not to look for in
them, and the parameters that their formats cannot find on their own.
.Pp
Hints are taken by the compressor alone: nothing of them is in the archive (but the files of
attributes, as files), whose formulas tell all that decoding needs.
A hint is a guess to try first, not a fact: a format told that turns the file down leaves
it to detection, and a wrong hint costs size and time alone.
Hints never turn on what
.Fl -lossy
alone does.
.Ss Where they are read
In the order of their precedence, the first winning:
.Bl -enum
.It
The files of
.Fl -attributes ,
in the order given: their patterns from the root of each input, the files their values name
relative to their own directories.
.It
.Pa .aqr/attributes
at the root of each input that is a directory (not under it: one file tells of the whole
tree); the files its values name relative to that root.
Its patterns may step into containers
.Pq see Sx Members of containers .
.It
The
.Pa .gitattributes
of each directory under an input that is a directory, as
.Xr git 1
reads them (those of deeper directories first); only
.Ql aqr-
attributes and a few of git's own
.Pq see Sx Attributes of git
are read.
.It
What the bytes and the name of the file tell.
.El
.Pp
Within a file, a later line wins, and within a line, a later attribute.
An input that is a single file has no
.Pa .aqr/attributes
nor
.Pa .gitattributes
(nothing above it is looked for: a file out of the inputs would change what is made);
.Fl -attributes
still applies.
With
.Fl a ,
those of the inputs appended are read.
.Ss Syntax
That of
.Xr gitattributes 5 :
a line each, of a pattern and attributes after it, by spaces:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
PATTERN attr -attr !attr attr=value ...
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql attr
sets an attribute,
.Ql -attr
unsets it,
.Ql !attr
makes it unspecified (as if no line of less precedence told it), and
.Ql attr=value
gives it a value (of no spaces).
A line starting with
.Ql #
and a blank line are left out.
.Pp
The patterns are those of
.Pa .gitignore :
one of no
.Ql /
(but at its end) matches a base name at any depth; else it is a path from the directory of
the file (or the root of the input, for
.Pa .aqr/attributes
and
.Fl -attributes ) ,
a leading
.Ql /
taken off.
.Ql *
and
.Ql \&?
match any bytes and one byte but
.Ql / ,
.Ql [...]
one of a set,
.Ql **
as a component of its own any directories, and
.Ql \e
escapes a byte.
A pattern ending in
.Ql /
matches directories, so no file: write
.Ql dir/** .
There are no negative patterns
.Pq Ql \&! ;
their lines are left out, as git does.
A pattern may be quoted as C strings are
.Pq Ql \(dqa b.bin\(dq .
Names are matched as they are, case and all, on every system.
.Pp
.Ql [attr] Ns Ar name Ar attr ...
defines a macro: a line setting
.Ar name
sets the attributes after it.
Macros are defined in
.Pa .aqr/attributes ,
the files of
.Fl -attributes
and the
.Pa .gitattributes
at the root of an input.
.Ql binary
is git's
.Pq Ql -diff -merge -text .
.Ss Members of containers
In
.Pa .aqr/attributes
and the files of
.Fl -attributes ,
.Ql >
steps into a container: what is before it is a path of the tree, what is after it a path from
the root of the container, each part matched on its own as above.
A container is what
.Nm aqr
takes apart into members of names: ZIP, tar, 7z, ALZ and EGG archives, asar archives, the
file systems of disk images, packs of games and the like.
Of a stream of no names (gzip, xz, Zstandard), the part after
.Ql >
is empty: the one thing it holds.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
loupe.zip>*.bin            aqr-format=tensors aqr-dtype=f16
game/*.pak>sounds/**       aqr-kind=compressed
backup.tar.zst>>db/*.dat   aqr-format=sqlite
.Ed
.Pp
A pattern of no
.Ql >
matches no member of a container: each depth wants a line of its own.
A name with
.Ql >
in it is written
.Ql \e> .
In
.Pa .gitattributes ,
.Ql >
is a byte of names as any other, and its lines tell of the files of the tree alone.
.Sh ATTRIBUTES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic aqr-format Ns = Ns Ar name
The file is of format
.Ar name ,
from its first byte on: it is tried first, not detected, and where it turns the file down,
the file is left to detection (the format its name tells too).
The names are those that
.Ql aqr -Z format:help
lists
.Po
.Cm zlib ,
.Cm png ,
.Cm sqlite ,
.Cm safetensors ,
\&...
.Pc ,
among them those of data of no header, which are never detected:
.Cm tensors ,
.Cm raw
(or
.Cm pcm )
and
.Cm deflate .
.It Fl aqr-format
The file is of no format: not detected, nor taken by its name.
.It Ic aqr-kind Ns = Ns Ar kind
What the file is, where its format is not told:
.Cm unknown ,
.Cm text ,
.Cm pixels ,
.Cm font ,
.Cm table ,
or
.Cm compressed
(encrypted, or compressed by a format no recompressor takes: nothing is tried).
.It Ic aqr-not Ns = Ns Ar name Ns Op , Ns Ar name ...
The formats not looked for in the file (as
.Fl Z Cm format:no- Ns Ar name
does for all files).
.It Ic aqr-offset Ns = Ns Ar n
With
.Ic aqr-format :
the file is of the format from byte
.Ar n
on (decimal, or hexadecimal after
.Ql 0x ) ,
the bytes before it taken as any other data.
.El
.Ss Parameters of formats
An
.Ic aqr- Ns Ar param Ns = Ns Ar value
of no other meaning is a parameter of the format that
.Ic aqr-format
tells, read of the files of that format alone.
A value the format does not take is told and the hint left (the file to detection).
.Pp
.Cm tensors ,
arrays of numbers of no header:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic aqr-dtype Ns = Ns Ar type
The whole file a tensor of
.Ar type :
.Cm bf16 , f16 , f32 , f64 ,
.Cm e4m3 , e5m2
(floats of 8 bits), or
.Cm i8 , u8 , i16 , u16 , i32 , u32 , i64 , u64 .
The bytes after the last whole element are kept as they are.
.It Ic aqr-layout Ns = Ns Ar file
The tensors the file holds, as
.Ar file
tells them (a TOML file; see
.Sx Layouts ) .
Better than
.Ic aqr-dtype
alone, which takes the file as one tensor.
.El
.Pp
.Cm raw ,
PCM samples of no header (taken as
.Cm pcm
where any of these is given):
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic aqr-bits Ns = Ns Cm 8 | 16 | 24 | 32
Bits of a sample (16).
.It Ic aqr-channels Ns = Ns Ar n
Channels (2).
.It Ic aqr-endian Ns = Ns Cm le | be
Byte order
.Pq Cm le .
.It Ic aqr-sign Ns = Ns Cm signed | unsigned
Signed, but unsigned of 8 bits.
.El
.Ss Layouts
The file of
.Ic aqr-layout ,
by convention under
.Pa .aqr/
(archived with the rest), relative to the root of the input where
.Pa .aqr/attributes
names it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dtype = "f16"              # where a tensor gives none

[[tensor]]
name = "conv1.weight"      # left out
shape = [64, 3, 7, 7]      # outermost first; or count = 9408
offset = 0                 # bytes; else where the one before ends

[[tensor]]
shape = [64]
dtype = "f32"
.Ed
.Ss Attributes of git
Of
.Pa .gitattributes
alone, and weaker than what the bytes tell:
.Ql binary
and
.Ql -text
(not text: text is not looked for),
.Ql text ,
.Ql eol= Ns Ar ...
and
.Ql filter=lfs
(text).
.Sh EXAMPLES
Weights of models, as arrays of 16-bit floats, in the tree and in a ZIP archive of it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[attr]f16-tensors aqr-format=tensors aqr-dtype=f16
*_f16.bin            f16-tensors
loupe.zip>*_f16.bin  f16-tensors
.Ed
.Pp
A zlib stream after a header of 16 bytes, and audio of no header:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
assets/*.pkd         aqr-format=zlib aqr-offset=16
sounds/*.raw         aqr-format=raw aqr-bits=16 aqr-channels=1
.Ed
.Pp
Encrypted files, and files that only look like ZIP archives:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
secrets/**           aqr-kind=compressed
*.dat                aqr-not=zip
.Ed
.Pp
Hints from outside a tree that cannot be changed:
.Dl $ aqr --attributes hints.txt -o game.aqr game
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Nm aqr Fl v
and
.Fl -stats
tell the attributes not understood, the files a format told turned down (or made no
smaller), and the lines that matched no file; so does the
.Ql hints
of
.Fl -stats Ns = Ns Ar file Ns .json .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr aqr 1 ,
.Xr aqrsfx 5 ,
.Xr gitattributes 5 ,
.Xr gitignore 5
